1
00:00:00,937 --> 00:00:03,940
In this video, we're going to
do an introduction to buildroot.

2
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:08,304
So, learning objectives, we'll talk about
build systems generically, what they are,

3
00:00:08,304 --> 00:00:11,151
and how we'll use them for
embedded systems development.

4
00:00:11,151 --> 00:00:15,740
And then we'll give an overview of
buildroot as a specific build system tool.

5
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:19,604
So we talked before about the Linux
components that are common to embedded

6
00:00:19,604 --> 00:00:24,044
systems, the toolchain, the bootloader,
the kernel, and the root filesystem.

7
00:00:24,044 --> 00:00:29,012
So how are we going to generate all
these components that we need for

8
00:00:29,012 --> 00:00:30,770
our embedded system?

9
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:32,655
There are several options to do this.

10
00:00:32,655 --> 00:00:37,455
One would be a build system,
an example of that would be Buildroot,

11
00:00:37,455 --> 00:00:38,990
Yocto, or OpenWRT.

12
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:42,095
We're going to talk in this
class about Buildroot or Yocto.

13
00:00:42,095 --> 00:00:46,646
Another option would be Roll Your Own, you
could essentially use kind of like cross

14
00:00:46,646 --> 00:00:49,590
tool, NG or
some tool like that to build a toolchain.

15
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:53,174
You could build your own root
file system from scratch,

16
00:00:53,174 --> 00:00:58,446
you can basically do all these steps on
your own without a dedicated build system.

17
00:00:58,446 --> 00:01:01,830
And the third way is you could
use some existing distribution

18
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:03,488
that already builds these for

19
00:01:03,488 --> 00:01:08,011
you or that already has specific versions
of these that it puts together for you.

20
00:01:08,011 --> 00:01:09,612
So for instance, debian or Ubuntu.

21
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:16,600
So why would you use a build system
instead of Rolling Your Own?

22
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:21,401
And really the question would be,
why would you need to reinvent the wheel?

23
00:01:21,401 --> 00:01:26,275
What would be the benefit of putting the
work in to Roll Your Own build system when

24
00:01:26,275 --> 00:01:28,691
they're already are ones available?

25
00:01:28,691 --> 00:01:33,430
So, you need to make sure that there's a
really specific reason that you need to be

26
00:01:33,430 --> 00:01:36,200
doing that, and typically, there won't be.

27
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,252
So it's usually,
it's going to be a lot more efficient for

28
00:01:39,252 --> 00:01:41,987
you to learn one of these
existing build systems and

29
00:01:41,987 --> 00:01:45,244
use it than it would be to
Roll Your Own custom build systems.

30
00:01:45,244 --> 00:01:49,243
So really the main reason I would
think of to use for Roll Your Own is

31
00:01:49,243 --> 00:01:53,460
the reason that we did to kind of
just learn the different parts of it,

32
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:57,440
but I would not use Roll Your Own for
a production project.

33
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,514
Another important thing to think about is,
what is your strategy for

34
00:02:00,514 --> 00:02:02,110
keeping up with security patches?

35
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:05,405
And so, if you're Rolling Your Own OS and
you're building every one of your

36
00:02:05,405 --> 00:02:08,456
applications, downloading them all,
building them from scratch.

37
00:02:08,456 --> 00:02:11,636
You should think about what's going to
happen when there's security patch

38
00:02:11,636 --> 00:02:14,262
available for one of those
applications I just downloaded.

39
00:02:14,262 --> 00:02:18,839
Am I going to watch the CV reports for
every single one of the applications I'm

40
00:02:18,839 --> 00:02:23,240
including in my root file system,
try to manage that all myself?

41
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:26,321
That's probably going to
be a pretty big task, and

42
00:02:26,321 --> 00:02:31,355
this is pointing you to a reason why you
should try to use a different strategy for

43
00:02:31,355 --> 00:02:33,926
your embedded Linux buildroot system.

44
00:02:33,926 --> 00:02:40,320
So then talking about using a build
system versus using a distribution.

45
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:45,449
And some of the trade offs between using
a distribution over a build system.

46
00:02:45,449 --> 00:02:49,865
So the first thing to know about if you're
going to use something like debian instead

47
00:02:49,865 --> 00:02:52,810
of using a build system to
build your embedded system.

48
00:02:52,810 --> 00:02:56,968
You need to think about the fact that
the goals of the distribution are probably

49
00:02:56,968 --> 00:02:59,243
different than of your embedded project.

50
00:02:59,243 --> 00:03:03,561
So the distribution is probably built and
focused on users and desktops.

51
00:03:03,561 --> 00:03:07,758
And so, a lot of the decisions that are
made about packaging that distribution,

52
00:03:07,758 --> 00:03:09,362
are going to be based on that, and

53
00:03:09,362 --> 00:03:12,471
that might not map very well
to your embedded Linux system.

54
00:03:12,471 --> 00:03:16,732
For instance, you might have
a headless system that doesn't even

55
00:03:16,732 --> 00:03:21,161
have a UI display or any type of
user interaction that's occurring.

56
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:26,503
So a lot of times, things like upgrades,
if you go through a distribution upgrade,

57
00:03:26,503 --> 00:03:28,563
it will ask you a bunch
of questions about,

58
00:03:28,563 --> 00:03:30,640
do you want to keep
this old configuration?

59
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:33,311
What's your username and password?

60
00:03:33,311 --> 00:03:36,292
What your time zone, for instance,
when you're doing your installs?

61
00:03:36,292 --> 00:03:39,798
So upgrades and installs a lot of time
we're going to presume someone's going to

62
00:03:39,798 --> 00:03:41,067
interact with the system.

63
00:03:41,067 --> 00:03:44,925
And you're going to have to fix all those
as a part of if that assumption is not

64
00:03:44,925 --> 00:03:46,806
correct for your embedded system.

65
00:03:46,806 --> 00:03:50,978
And also the package management for
distribution is not really designed for

66
00:03:50,978 --> 00:03:52,890
embedded software development.

67
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:57,348
Again, there's a different goal in mind
here of who the distribution is based on.

68
00:03:57,348 --> 00:04:00,256
So a lot, you're going to have
to find those problems and

69
00:04:00,256 --> 00:04:02,861
fix them to work better
with your embedded work.

70
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:08,484
Another few things to think about if
you're using a distribution instead

71
00:04:08,484 --> 00:04:10,530
of a build system, in a distribution,

72
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:12,887
reducing the image size
may be challenging.

73
00:04:12,887 --> 00:04:17,398
And again, the goal of the distribution
is to make it easy for

74
00:04:17,398 --> 00:04:22,084
users to use it and it's probably
not to fit on a very small flash

75
00:04:22,084 --> 00:04:27,940
device that isn't a common use
scenario for a PC, for instance.

76
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:31,304
And you'll also likely to do
some customization work to

77
00:04:31,304 --> 00:04:33,172
generate a production image.

78
00:04:33,172 --> 00:04:37,541
The workflow for a distribution is
usually installed from a USB image and

79
00:04:37,541 --> 00:04:41,430
then install a whole bunch of
applications on top to customize.

80
00:04:41,430 --> 00:04:44,955
Whereas, what you want from an embedded
system perspective is you want to

81
00:04:44,955 --> 00:04:48,304
build a whole root file system that
you can just flash onto a device and

82
00:04:48,304 --> 00:04:51,169
production that's got all
your custom components on it.

83
00:04:51,169 --> 00:04:53,715
So that's possible with the distribution,
but

84
00:04:53,715 --> 00:04:57,250
it's going to take a little bit more
work for you to get that all set up,

85
00:04:57,250 --> 00:05:00,061
this is something that a build
system will do for you.

86
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:05,554
Another thing to think about where the
distribution is that the way distribution

87
00:05:05,554 --> 00:05:09,617
upgrades work is that they upgrade
individual packages from one division to

88
00:05:09,617 --> 00:05:10,201
another.

89
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:14,090
And those individual upgrades may
work slightly different when you're

90
00:05:14,090 --> 00:05:17,840
going between two different
revisions of the application.

91
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:21,126
And so, you're not just swapping out
one application with with another,

92
00:05:21,126 --> 00:05:24,166
you're running an upgrade on every
single one of the applications.

93
00:05:24,166 --> 00:05:27,993
And so, what this means is that you can
have subtle differences in your file

94
00:05:27,993 --> 00:05:32,031
system that happened depending on how
you upgraded from one point to another.

95
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:36,980
So, say I went from version 1
to version 3 of my distribution,

96
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:41,012
or I went from version 1
to version 2 to version 3,

97
00:05:41,012 --> 00:05:45,162
there might be some slight
differences in that path.

98
00:05:45,162 --> 00:05:49,433
That means that you end up with slightly
different content in your profile system,

99
00:05:49,433 --> 00:05:52,750
and this could cause subtle and
difficult errors to track down.

100
00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:56,578
So this is another thing to keep in mind
when you're making this decision about,

101
00:05:56,578 --> 00:05:58,511
do I use a build system or distribution?

102
00:05:58,511 --> 00:06:01,061
And the reason why you might want to
consider using a build system.

103
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:06,812
So, from here on out, we're going to
assume that you're using a build system

104
00:06:06,812 --> 00:06:11,019
for your embedded system builds, and
we're going to talk about buildroot,

105
00:06:11,019 --> 00:06:13,308
Yocto has two different ways to do that.

106
00:06:13,308 --> 00:06:14,680
And in this particular video,

107
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:17,840
we're going to talk
specifically about buildroot.

108
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:19,856
So the steps that are going to perform for

109
00:06:19,856 --> 00:06:23,951
you by your build system that in this
part will be generic to any build system,

110
00:06:23,951 --> 00:06:27,432
it's going to download source for
common packages from upstream.

111
00:06:27,432 --> 00:06:31,236
So, we talked about filling the root
file system with applications,

112
00:06:31,236 --> 00:06:35,168
those applications are all going to
come from open source repositories and

113
00:06:35,168 --> 00:06:37,301
developers that are working on these.

114
00:06:37,301 --> 00:06:41,630
And every one of those is going to have
some source code storage somewhere online.

115
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:45,103
And your build system is going to
know where all those locations are,

116
00:06:45,103 --> 00:06:49,011
it's going to download all the source,
and it's going to apply the patches for

117
00:06:49,011 --> 00:06:50,145
cross compilation.

118
00:06:50,145 --> 00:06:53,896
Maybe fix some specific bugs that
are known, but the given release, and

119
00:06:53,896 --> 00:06:55,814
then it's going to build them for you.

120
00:06:55,814 --> 00:06:59,528
And it's going to take the output of
the build and it's going to put it in

121
00:06:59,528 --> 00:07:03,740
the staging area that will ultimately
turn into your file system.

122
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:06,791
It's going to use that staging area to
create the image files that you can then

123
00:07:06,791 --> 00:07:08,010
put on the device and deploy.

124
00:07:08,010 --> 00:07:14,037
A few other things that are handy
that you'll get with a build system,

125
00:07:14,037 --> 00:07:18,749
it's going to allow you to
easily add your own packages.

126
00:07:18,749 --> 00:07:24,783
And so, your own packages are probably
coming from your own repositories

127
00:07:24,783 --> 00:07:31,027
that may not be public, and it's going to
have a way for you to add that easily.

128
00:07:31,027 --> 00:07:34,499
And it allows you to use
a proprietary license, for

129
00:07:34,499 --> 00:07:37,430
instance, for some of the applications.

130
00:07:37,430 --> 00:07:41,035
And so, the source code might
not be distributed for those and

131
00:07:41,035 --> 00:07:45,193
you might need special access,
you might need to build inside a VPN, or

132
00:07:45,193 --> 00:07:48,060
you might need an ssh key
to access that content.

133
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:51,351
And your build system is
going to be able to support that.

134
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:57,932
It's also going to let you select system
profiles for your application target.

135
00:07:57,932 --> 00:08:01,332
So for instance,
if you're building a headless system and

136
00:08:01,332 --> 00:08:06,092
you don't need graphics support, then
there'll be something in the build system

137
00:08:06,092 --> 00:08:09,711
that will allow you to select whether or
not you need graphics.

138
00:08:09,711 --> 00:08:13,127
So this is a really easy way that you
could stripped down the image size for

139
00:08:13,127 --> 00:08:15,483
a system that doesn't
need any graphic support.

140
00:08:15,483 --> 00:08:19,308
You can just turn that off in
the profile of the build system,

141
00:08:19,308 --> 00:08:24,185
and you free up a bunch of root filesystem
space that would in the distribution

142
00:08:24,185 --> 00:08:27,351
potentially be used and
not necessarily needed.

143
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:32,809
A build system is also going to track
the open source licenses that are used in

144
00:08:32,809 --> 00:08:33,623
the project.

145
00:08:33,623 --> 00:08:34,555
And remember,

146
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:38,575
we talked about your requirements
in use of open source licenses.

147
00:08:38,575 --> 00:08:43,274
And the fact that this means that you
have some responsibilities to share

148
00:08:43,274 --> 00:08:47,064
the source code that's
associated with those licenses.

149
00:08:47,064 --> 00:08:49,948
And the build system is
going to help you with that, so

150
00:08:49,948 --> 00:08:53,452
it's going to track which licenses
are used in which packages.

151
00:08:53,452 --> 00:08:57,497
And it's going to help you create some
content that you can use to share and

152
00:08:57,497 --> 00:08:59,461
meet your license requirements.

153
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:04,162
So talking specifically about buildroot,
what is buildroot?

154
00:09:04,162 --> 00:09:07,611
Buildroot is one of the build systems
that we're going to talk about and

155
00:09:07,611 --> 00:09:11,595
use this semester, and buildroot is kind
of like you would guess from the name,

156
00:09:11,595 --> 00:09:14,720
it's a builder of remote file systems for
embedded devices.

157
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:19,097
So it's really a collection of Linux
packages with build instructions, and

158
00:09:19,097 --> 00:09:21,736
that includes both host and
target packages.

159
00:09:21,736 --> 00:09:24,706
Meaning that we're going to
install libraries and

160
00:09:24,706 --> 00:09:29,131
applications on our host that are going to
help us build and cross compile.

161
00:09:29,131 --> 00:09:32,289
So like the cross compiled tools
that we installed manually,

162
00:09:32,289 --> 00:09:35,886
that's all going to be installed for
us as a part of the build up package.

163
00:09:35,886 --> 00:09:38,551
And then it's going to be packages
that are intended for the target.

164
00:09:38,551 --> 00:09:41,744
So content that we're going to
cross compile in our host and

165
00:09:41,744 --> 00:09:45,877
set up in our root file system,
ultimately to be installed in the target.

166
00:09:45,877 --> 00:09:50,282
Buildroot was started back in 2001,
so it's been around a very long time,

167
00:09:50,282 --> 00:09:53,058
and one of buildroot main
focuses on simplicity.

168
00:09:53,058 --> 00:09:58,134
And that's the reason we start with
it in this course, because it's

169
00:09:58,134 --> 00:10:03,489
the definitely the simpler of the two
to get up to speed and to start with.

170
00:10:03,489 --> 00:10:08,073
So the buildroot build system is
licensed GPLv2, so if you remember our

171
00:10:08,073 --> 00:10:12,521
discussion about the different
licensing for open source software.

172
00:10:12,521 --> 00:10:17,925
GPLv2 means that you're expected to share
changes to the source with the community,

173
00:10:17,925 --> 00:10:23,271
and that's part of what the licenses your
responsibilities for using that license.

174
00:10:23,271 --> 00:10:26,879
It leverages the popular and
ubiquitous make files and

175
00:10:26,879 --> 00:10:29,090
K kernel kconfig, menuconfig.

176
00:10:29,090 --> 00:10:33,250
So what we've already talked about, what
we've already used to build the kernel,

177
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:36,368
what we already used to build and
configure busybox, ct-ng.

178
00:10:36,368 --> 00:10:40,540
That same configuration mechanism
is going to be used by buildroot.

179
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:44,044
So another reason that this
one is kind of easy for

180
00:10:44,044 --> 00:10:48,492
you to get started if you only
have experience with that tool.

181
00:10:48,492 --> 00:10:53,082
So when you're installing building,
it's really based on the new make and

182
00:10:53,082 --> 00:10:57,887
a few utilities that are detailed on the
requirements page here that you can find

183
00:10:57,887 --> 00:10:59,340
links below.

184
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:04,102
You'll need a collection of
packages installed in your host and

185
00:11:04,102 --> 00:11:07,726
there's a link to
the mandatory packages here.

186
00:11:07,726 --> 00:11:12,990
And I also suggest and curses that will
just let you be able to use menuconfig.

187
00:11:12,990 --> 00:11:18,380
And for buildroot, this is the end curses
library that you can install here.

188
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:21,454
So buildroot refers to
packages as the definition for

189
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:25,700
the different things that we're going to
be adding to the root file system.

190
00:11:25,700 --> 00:11:29,759
Basically, the source we're
going to pull down and compile, and

191
00:11:29,759 --> 00:11:34,649
then the instructions were putting that
cross compiled content into our root

192
00:11:34,649 --> 00:11:35,566
file system.

193
00:11:35,566 --> 00:11:39,568
The building install instructions
are going to use a GNU Make syntax, and

194
00:11:39,568 --> 00:11:42,303
so you're essentially
going to be building head or

195
00:11:42,303 --> 00:11:45,639
include files that will be
included in the new make files and

196
00:11:45,639 --> 00:11:49,940
describe what needs to get done
in order to build your packages.

197
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:52,093
And all of the built in packages for

198
00:11:52,093 --> 00:11:57,151
buildroot are going to be located in the
buildroot tree under a package directory.

199
00:11:57,151 --> 00:12:02,257
And buildroot also supports what's called
br2-external trees, so you can have your

200
00:12:02,257 --> 00:12:07,231
own tree that you define for your package
builds that happened outside of buildroot.

201
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:11,800
So why might you want to
use a br2-external tree?

202
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:14,071
And why would be used in this course?

203
00:12:14,071 --> 00:12:18,139
It's because in pretty much every
application you're going to implement,

204
00:12:18,139 --> 00:12:21,450
you'll have some packages that
you can't share upstream.

205
00:12:21,450 --> 00:12:24,603
In other words, you're going to
have some packages that you build,

206
00:12:24,603 --> 00:12:26,753
they're going to have
a proprietary license.

207
00:12:26,753 --> 00:12:30,218
Most likely, you'll have
applications you're developing for

208
00:12:30,218 --> 00:12:33,557
your embedded system that you
are going to want to share publicly.

209
00:12:33,557 --> 00:12:38,679
And so, you'd want to use an external tree
to share that content because it's not

210
00:12:38,679 --> 00:12:43,594
something that you're going to be
contributing back to buildroot community.

211
00:12:43,594 --> 00:12:47,241
So packages can reference get
repositories for source code, and

212
00:12:47,241 --> 00:12:51,552
you can build your own custom application
from dedicated repositories, and

213
00:12:51,552 --> 00:12:54,951
we'll show an example of how to
do that with assignment for.

214
00:12:56,440 --> 00:13:01,363
So every package definition needs at
least two files with some special

215
00:13:01,363 --> 00:13:05,953
syntax that's defined by
the buildroot project that config.in

216
00:13:05,953 --> 00:13:10,722
is KConfig framework code that adds
the package to the config menu.

217
00:13:10,722 --> 00:13:14,861
So when you use menuconfig you can see
your package that's going to be defined in

218
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:15,640
config.in.

219
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:19,315
And then there's also
the package_name>.mk file,

220
00:13:19,315 --> 00:13:24,318
which is where you're recipe editions and
your variables that you define,

221
00:13:24,318 --> 00:13:28,475
and can you make syntax are going to
be used to build your package.

222
00:13:28,475 --> 00:13:31,402
So the buildroot package definitions
don't contain your code,

223
